```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter a number: ");
        int n = scanner.nextInt();
        
        String str1 = "10^" + String.valueOf(n) + "^(";
        String str2 = "10000+";
        String str3 = "-5*";
        String str4 = "a";

        System.out.println(str1 + "The value of 'a' is: ");
        System.out.println(str2 + "The value of 'a' is: ");
        System.out.println(str3 + "The value of 'a' is: ");
        System.out.println(str4 + "The value of 'a' is: ");

        // Test cases
        System.out.println(testCase(str1, n));
        System.out.println(testCase(str2, n));
        System.out.println(testCase(str3, n));
        System.out.println(testCase(str4, n));
    }

    public static int testCase(String str, int n) {
        String baseStr = "10^" + String.valueOf(n);
        return (int) Math.pow(10000, 5 - Integer.parseInt(baseStr.split("\\^")[0].split("^")[1]));
    }
}
```

In this solution, the `testCase` function calculates the value of 'a' as per the formula `(10000 * base)^(-n)`. This formula is derived from the concept that for any integer 'base', the number with base 'base' raised to power (5-n) will be equal to a certain fixed number.
